The flat neighbourhood system of natural numbers, with successor, predecessor, and zero-test as approximable maps.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`memN`).** A subset of the naturals is a neighbourhood precisely when it is the universe or a singleton.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`memN_singleton`).** Every singleton is a neighbourhood.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`memN_univ`).** The universe is a neighbourhood.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`nestedOrDisjoint`).** Any two neighbourhoods are nested or disjoint.

*Proof.* Case on the neighbourhood predicates: universals nest; a singleton nests in the universe; distinct singletons are disjoint. ∎

**Definition (`N`).** The number domain is the neighbourhood system on the naturals generated by that nested-or-disjoint predicate with universe as master.

*Proof.* Assemble the nested-or-disjoint instance. ∎

**Theorem (`N_mem`).** Membership in the number domain is the neighbourhood predicate.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`N_master`).** The master neighbourhood is the universe.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`T`).** The two-token domain of truth values is the ambient truth domain.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`natElem`).** The numeral of a natural is the principal ideal of its singleton.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`predVal`).** The predecessor value sends zero to bottom and a successor numeral to the preceding numeral.

*Proof.* By recursion on the natural. ∎

**Theorem (`singleton_nat_inj`).** Equal singletons determine equal naturals.

*Proof.* Membership of the left witness in the right singleton. ∎

**Theorem (`univ_ne_singleton`).** The universe is not a singleton.

*Proof.* The successor of the singleton’s element lies in the universe but not in the singleton. ∎

**Theorem (`singleton_ne_univ`).** A singleton is not the universe.

*Proof.* Symmetry of the previous inequality. ∎

**Definition (`constLiftN`).** A value assignment on naturals lifts to an approximable map from the number domain by relating the universe to the target master and each singleton to neighbourhoods of the assigned value.

*Proof.* Domain and codomain membership unpack the two clauses; the masters relate via the universe clause; right intersection and monotonicity case on those clauses using the singleton–universe inequalities. ∎

**Definition (`predMap`).** Predecessor is the constant lift of the predecessor value.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`succMap`).** Successor is the constant lift of the successor-numeral assignment.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`zeroElt`).** Zero as an ideal element is the numeral of zero.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`zeroVal`).** The zero-test value sends zero to true and positive naturals to false.

*Proof.* By recursion on the natural. ∎

**Definition (`zeroMap`).** The zero-test map is the constant lift of the zero-test value into the truth domain.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`N_bot_mem`).** The bottom element of the number domain contains precisely the universe.

*Proof.* Invoke the general bottom-membership law. ∎

**Theorem (`constLiftN_bot`).** A constant lift sends bottom to the target bottom.

*Proof.* Compare membership: a witnessing neighbourhood of bottom must be the universe, which forces the target master; conversely the target bottom is witnessed by the universe clause. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_natElem_iff`).** A neighbourhood belongs to a numeral precisely when it is the universe or that numeral’s singleton.

*Proof.* Unpack principal membership and the neighbourhood predicate. ∎

**Theorem (`constLiftN_natElem`).** A constant lift evaluates at a numeral to the assigned value.

*Proof.* Compare membership using the numeral-membership characterisation and the singleton clause of the lift. ∎

**Theorem (`predMap_bot`).** Predecessor sends bottom to bottom.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift bottom law. ∎

**Theorem (`predMap_natElem_succ`).** Predecessor of a successor numeral is the preceding numeral.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift evaluation law. ∎

**Theorem (`predMap_natElem_zero`).** Predecessor of the zero numeral is bottom.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift evaluation law. ∎

**Theorem (`succMap_bot`).** Successor sends bottom to bottom.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift bottom law. ∎

**Theorem (`succMap_natElem`).** Successor of a numeral is the next numeral.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift evaluation law. ∎

**Theorem (`predMap_succMap_natElem`).** Predecessor after successor recovers a numeral.

*Proof.* Compose the successor and predecessor evaluation laws. ∎

**Theorem (`zeroMap_bot`).** The zero-test sends bottom to the truth-domain bottom.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift bottom law. ∎

**Theorem (`zeroMap_natElem_succ`).** The zero-test of a positive numeral is false.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift evaluation law. ∎

**Theorem (`zeroMap_natElem_zero`).** The zero-test of the zero numeral is true.

*Proof.* Invoke the constant-lift evaluation law. ∎
